 "notes": "Michelsberg culture (c. 4400-3500 BC, Rhine land), typed culture like the Linear Pottery and Lengyel dossiers; Cliopatria has no row. Three pages opened.\n\nTo settle: (1) The page gives no dates for the spread beyond the emergence in northeastern France; the Rhine land is drawn from -4300, the end of the Rössen (confidence 1), though the Rössen page says Michelsberg complexes appear in its later period; the real spread was probably earlier and gradual. (2) Overlap with the rossen-culture shape in -4400..-4300 is limited to northeastern France, because the Rhine land is gained in -4300, the year the Rössen ends. (3) Followers without dossiers: wartberg-culture (3,600-2,800 BC; the Michelsberg ends 3,500, so they overlap 100 years), pfyn-culture (infobox only, no years read), and the Cerny culture of the Paris Basin (predecessor). The funnelbeaker-culture dossier (-4100) lies to the north and records no hand-over from the Michelsberg although that dossier quotes its page as saying it 'originated through a migration of colonists from the Michelsberg culture'; the Michelsberg page says Michelsberg colonists played an instrumental role in establishing it. (4) Michelsberg also 'has close affinities to the cultures of the Neolithic British Isles' and 'strong affinities to the Chasséen culture of central France' (Chasséen 4500-3500 BC in a caption): neither is territory. (5) Native name Michelsberger Kultur is the standard German name, not read from the page text returned. (6) Gazetteer names used (Lorraine, Alsace, Champagne, Kassel and the admin1 units) all resolve."
